        "question": {
          ":question/id": "b22e5181-5266-53ec-ad1f-b8449fbd42ae",
          ":question/problem": "Consider the plane curve $- 2 x^{2} - x y - 3 y=-10$. Find a tangent vector to the curve at the point $(-2,2)$.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "Set $f(x,y)=- 2 x^{2} - x y - 3 y$. The curve is the level curve $f(x,y)=-10$, and $f(-2,2)=-10$ confirms that the point lies on it.\n\nA tangent vector is perpendicular to the nonzero gradient. Use $\\mathbf t=f_y\\mathbf i-f_x\\mathbf j$. Differentiate while holding the other variable constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y)&=- 4 x - y\\\\ f_y(x,y)&=- x - 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the supplied point,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(-2,2)&=- 4 \\left(-2\\right) - \\left(2\\right)\\\\ &=6\\\\ f_y(-2,2)&=- \\left(-2\\right) - 3\\\\ &=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus one tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf t(-2,2)=-\\mathbf i-6\\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero. The dot product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(-2,2)\\cdot\\mathbf t(-2,2)&=\\left(6\\right)\\left(-1\\right)\\\\ &\\quad-\\left(-1\\right)\\left(6\\right)\\\\ &=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAny nonzero scalar multiple has the same tangent direction; none of the other listed vectors is perpendicular to this gradient.",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
