          ":question/id": "3c4bb4a3-3a51-5abb-9494-3072918dbaf6",
          ":question/problem": "Consider the plane curve $- 2 x^{2} + x - y^{2} - y=-16$. Find a tangent vector to the curve at the point $(-2,2)$.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "Set $f(x,y)=- 2 x^{2} + x - y^{2} - y$. The curve is the level curve $f(x,y)=-16$, and $f(-2,2)=-16$ confirms that the point lies on it.\n\nA tangent vector is perpendicular to the nonzero gradient. Use $\\mathbf t=f_y\\mathbf i-f_x\\mathbf j$. Differentiate while holding the other variable constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y)&=1 - 4 x\\\\ f_y(x,y)&=- 2 y - 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the supplied point,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(-2,2)&=1 - 4 \\left(-2\\right)\\\\ &=9\\\\ f_y(-2,2)&=- 2 \\left(2\\right) - 1\\\\ &=-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus one tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf t(-2,2)=-5\\,\\mathbf i-9\\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nMultiplying by $-1$ gives the listed tangent vector $5\\,\\mathbf i+9\\,\\mathbf j$. The gradient is nonzero. The dot product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(-2,2)\\cdot\\mathbf t(-2,2)&=\\left(9\\right)\\left(-5\\right)\\\\ &\\quad-\\left(-5\\right)\\left(9\\right)\\\\ &=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAny nonzero scalar multiple has the same tangent direction; none of the other listed vectors is perpendicular to this gradient.",
