          ":question/problem": "Consider the plane curve $2 y e^{x y}=- x e^{y} + 3 e$. Find a tangent vector to the curve at the point $(1,1)$.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "Bring the variable terms to one side and set $f(x,y)=x e^{y} + 2 y e^{x y}$. The curve is the level curve $f(x,y)=3 e$, and $f(1,1)=3 e$ confirms that the point lies on it.\n\nA tangent vector is perpendicular to the nonzero gradient. Use $\\mathbf t=f_y\\mathbf i-f_x\\mathbf j$. Differentiate while holding the other variable constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y)&=2 y^{2} e^{x y} + e^{y}\\\\ f_y(x,y)&=2 x y e^{x y} + x e^{y} + 2 e^{x y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the supplied point,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(1,1)&=2 \\left(1\\right)^{2} e^{\\left(1\\right) \\left(1\\right)} + e^{\\left(1\\right)}\\\\ &=3 e\\\\ f_y(1,1)&=2 e^{\\left(1\\right) \\left(1\\right)}\\\\ &\\quad +\\left(1\\right) e^{\\left(1\\right)}\\\\ &\\quad +2 \\left(1\\right) \\left(1\\right) e^{\\left(1\\right) \\left(1\\right)}\\\\ &=5 e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus one tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf t(1,1)=5 e\\,\\mathbf i-3 e\\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero. The dot product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(1,1)\\cdot\\mathbf t(1,1)&=\\left(3 e\\right)\\left(5 e\\right)\\\\ &\\quad-\\left(5 e\\right)\\left(3 e\\right)\\\\ &=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAny nonzero scalar multiple has the same tangent direction; none of the other listed vectors is perpendicular to this gradient.",
