          ":question/problem": "Consider the plane curve $- 2 e^{x y}=x + 3 y - 4 - \\frac{2}{e^{4}}$. Find a tangent vector to the curve at the point $(-2,2)$.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "Bring the variable terms to one side and set $f(x,y)=- x - 3 y - 2 e^{x y}$. The curve is the level curve $f(x,y)=-4 - \\frac{2}{e^{4}}$, and $f(-2,2)=-4 - \\frac{2}{e^{4}}$ confirms that the point lies on it.\n\nA tangent vector is perpendicular to the nonzero gradient. Use $\\mathbf t=f_y\\mathbf i-f_x\\mathbf j$. Differentiate while holding the other variable constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y)&=- 2 y e^{x y} - 1\\\\ f_y(x,y)&=- 2 x e^{x y} - 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the supplied point,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(-2,2)&=- 2 \\left(2\\right) e^{\\left(-2\\right) \\left(2\\right)} - 1\\\\ &=-1 - \\frac{4}{e^{4}}\\\\ f_y(-2,2)&=- 2 \\left(-2\\right) e^{\\left(-2\\right) \\left(2\\right)} - 3\\\\ &=-3 + \\frac{4}{e^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus one tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf t(-2,2)=-\\left(3 - \\frac{4}{e^{4}}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+\\left(\\frac{4}{e^{4}} + 1\\right)\\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero. The dot product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(-2,2)\\cdot\\mathbf t(-2,2)&=\\left(-1 - \\frac{4}{e^{4}}\\right)\\left(-3 + \\frac{4}{e^{4}}\\right)\\\\ &\\quad-\\left(-3 + \\frac{4}{e^{4}}\\right)\\left(-1 - \\frac{4}{e^{4}}\\right)\\\\ &=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAny nonzero scalar multiple has the same tangent direction; none of the other listed vectors is perpendicular to this gradient.",
