          ":question/worked-solution": "Bring the variable terms to one side and set $f(x,y)=x^{2} - x y - 2 x \\ln{\\left(y \\right)}$. The curve is the level curve $f(x,y)=- e - 1$, and $f(1,e)=- e - 1$ confirms that the point lies on it.\n\nA tangent vector is perpendicular to the nonzero gradient. Use $\\mathbf t=f_y\\mathbf i-f_x\\mathbf j$. Differentiate while holding the other variable constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y)&=2 x - y - 2 \\ln{\\left(y \\right)}\\\\ f_y(x,y)&=- x - \\frac{2 x}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the supplied point,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(1,e)&=2 \\left(1\\right) - \\left(e\\right) - 2 \\ln{\\left(\\left(e\\right) \\right)}\\\\ &=- e\\\\ f_y(1,e)&=- \\left(1\\right) - \\frac{2 \\left(1\\right)}{\\left(e\\right)}\\\\ &=-1 - \\frac{2}{e}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus one tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf t(1,e)=-\\left(\\frac{2}{e} + 1\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+e\\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nMultiplying by $-1$ gives the listed tangent vector $\\left(\\frac{2}{e} + 1\\right)\\,\\mathbf i-e\\,\\mathbf j$. The gradient is nonzero. The dot product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(1,e)\\cdot\\mathbf t(1,e)&=\\left(- e\\right)\\left(-1 - \\frac{2}{e}\\right)\\\\ &\\quad-\\left(-1 - \\frac{2}{e}\\right)\\left(- e\\right)\\\\ &=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAny nonzero scalar multiple has the same tangent direction; none of the other listed vectors is perpendicular to this gradient.",
